# ---- Where the infrastructure lives ----------------------------------------
# Leave this set to run Postgres and Redis as local containers. Comment it out
# when DATABASE_URL / REDIS_URL below point at managed services, and those
# containers are never started.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=local-infra

# ---- Database -------------------------------------------------------------
# TWO roles, deliberately. The app connects as a role that does NOT own the
# tables, so PostgreSQL Row-Level Security applies to it; migrations connect as
# the owner. Pointing both at the same role makes that role the table owner, and
# a table owner BYPASSES ITS OWN RLS POLICIES — tenant isolation then stops
# applying with no error anywhere, while every test still passes. This is the
# single easiest thing to get wrong in this file.
#
# These are LEFT UNSET on purpose. `docker compose up` then uses the compose
# defaults, which address the local containers by service name (`db:5432`) — the
# only hostname that resolves from inside the API container.
#
# Uncomment these two ONLY if you run the API natively on the host
# (`cd apps/api && uv run uvicorn ...`), where `db` does not resolve:
#   DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://app_user:app_user_password@localhost:5432/saas_ai
#   MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://app_owner:app_owner_password@localhost:5432/saas_ai
#
# One `.env` cannot serve both at once while using local containers, because the
# two need different hostnames for the same database. Managed URLs do not have
# that problem — they resolve identically from the host and from a container,
# which is the main reason the managed setup is simpler to run.

# Managed Postgres (Neon, Supabase, Render, Heroku) — paste the URL as given.
# The scheme and libpq-only parameters (`sslmode`, `channel_binding`) are
# normalized at startup, so no hand-editing is needed. Create the two roles once
# against the managed instance; infrastructure/postgres/init.sql only ever runs
# against an empty LOCAL data directory, never against a managed database.
#   DATABASE_URL=postgresql://app_user:...@ep-xxx.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require
#   MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://app_owner:...@ep-xxx.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require

# ---- Redis ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Unset for the same reason as the database URLs above: compose defaults to
# `redis://redis:6379/0`, the service name reachable from the API container.
# Uncomment for a native run, or replace with a managed URL.
#   REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/0
# Managed (Upstash and friends) use TLS: rediss://default:<password>@<host>:6379

# ---- Security -------------------------------------------------------------
# Generate with: python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
JWT_SECRET=change-me-in-production
ACCESS_TOKEN_TTL_MINUTES=15
REFRESH_TOKEN_TTL_DAYS=30

# ---- App ------------------------------------------------------------------
ENVIRONMENT=local
LOG_LEVEL=INFO
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000

# ---- Web ------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8000
# Only needed when the Next server reaches the API at a different address than
# the browser does -- Docker Compose, where the browser uses the published host
# port above and the server has to use the service name. `docker-compose.yml`
# sets it for the `web` container; leave it unset everywhere else and the proxy
# rewrite falls back to NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL.
# API_INTERNAL_URL=http://api:8000

# ---- LLM providers ---------------------------------------------------------
# Set at least one of these. Creating an agent requires choosing a provider,
# and the dashboard only offers the ones whose key is set here -- with none
# set, the new-agent form says so and cannot be submitted.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# OpenRouter fronts many vendors behind OpenAI's API. Models whose id ends in
# `:free` cost nothing, at the price of a hard rate limit (~20 req/min, and
# 50 req/day until the account holds credits). Key from openrouter.ai/keys.
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# Only the development seed (`make seed`) reads this -- it is the provider its
# demo agent is created on. `fake` answers offline with a canned reply and
# needs no key, so `make seed` works on a fresh clone with nothing configured.
# It is NOT a default for agents you create: those always name their own
# provider, which is why every agent used to silently end up on `fake`.
DEFAULT_LLM_PROVIDER=fake

# ---- Knowledge base / RAG (Phase 3) ----------------------------------------
# Which embedding model ingestion and retrieval use. `hashing` is a real
# hashed bag-of-words embedder that needs no key and no network, so the
# pipeline works on a fresh clone; it is lexical, not semantic ("car" and
# "automobile" are unrelated to it). Set to `openai` once OPENAI_API_KEY is
# set above -- both emit 1536 dimensions, so the swap is a re-ingest of
# existing documents, not a schema migration. `docker-compose.yml` passes
# this through to both `api` and `worker`.
# EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=hashing

# Where uploaded document bytes wait between the upload request and the
# worker picking them up. Under compose this MUST resolve to the same path
# in both `api` and `worker` (the `uploads` volume is what makes it the same
# disk); leave it unset there. A local directory is a single-host
# placeholder -- see apps/api/app/rag/storage.py.
# UPLOAD_DIR=./var/uploads

# Ceiling on the whole `POST /api/v1/documents` request body, in bytes,
# including multipart overhead -- 20 MB by default. Put a matching (or
# smaller) body cap on the reverse proxy in front of the API too: this one
# rejects the request only after it has reached the application.
# MAX_REQUEST_BYTES=20971520

# Ingestion tuning. Larger batches ingest faster and put more chunks at risk
# of one failed request; retries are per batch, and a batch that exhausts
# them fails the whole document rather than storing a partial corpus.
# EMBEDDING_BATCH_SIZE=64
# EMBEDDING_MAX_RETRIES=3
# EMBEDDING_RETRY_BACKOFF_SECONDS=0.1

# How many ingest jobs the arq worker runs at once. Each holds up to two
# database connections, against a 15-connection pool -- raise this and
# `pool_size`/`max_overflow` in apps/api/app/db/session.py together.
# WORKER_MAX_JOBS=5

# The two relevance floors hybrid retrieval applies before fusing its two
# candidate lists. Without them every chat turn cited up to five chunks
# whatever the question was, because RRF scores come from rank position and
# carry no relevance information at all.
#
# RETRIEVAL_MAX_COSINE_DISTANCE is the vector arm's ceiling (0 = identical,
# 1 = unrelated). 0.8 was measured against the default `hashing` embedder on
# small test corpora, and it is a useful threshold rather than a clean
# dividing line: relevant and irrelevant queries land close together and DO
# overlap (0.556-0.860 against 0.792-1.000 across 33 queries), so some real
# questions fall above it and some filler falls below. It earns its place on
# the balance of outcomes -- 13 of 14 relevant queries still retrieve, 10 of
# 11 filler queries now retrieve nothing -- and because the keyword arm
# catches much of what it drops. A badly-fitted value here fails permissive
# (irrelevant chunks cited again), not silent (a corpus that stops
# answering). A real semantic embedder puts unrelated text far closer than
# 0.8, so re-measure this on any EMBEDDING_PROVIDER change rather than
# assuming it carries over.
#
# RETRIEVAL_MIN_KEYWORD_RANK is the `ts_rank_cd` floor on the keyword arm's
# OR fallback, which keeps a single incidental shared word from counting as
# grounding. Do not set it to 0: it is load-bearing for correctness, not
# just quality. A leading hyphen is negation to `websearch_to_tsquery`, so
# "-cat dog" falls back to `!'cat' | 'dog'`, which matches every chunk
# without "cat" -- the whole corpus -- and `ts_rank_cd` scores a negated
# match 0.0, so this floor is the only thing that drops it.
# RETRIEVAL_MAX_COSINE_DISTANCE=0.8
# RETRIEVAL_MIN_KEYWORD_RANK=0.15
